          "text": "{\"target_translation\":\"I love you, Your Majesty.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"Cordelia directly presents her love for Lear as true, then carefully defines its measure. This is a clear avowal, regardless of whether Lear finds it satisfactory.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":2,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.9,\"reason\":\"The expression invokes Cordelia's existing filial bond rather than making a new promise. She presents loving Lear as one of the duties she owes and returns as his daughter. Cordelia invokes that relational obligation; Lear does not accept this as an adequate response to his demand.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"According to my bond; no more nor less.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Return those duties back as are right fit,\\nObey you, love you, and most honour you.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Mend your speech a little,\\nLest you may mar your fortunes.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.97,\"reason\":\"The focal expression is a controlled, qualified answer, not a reflex-like affective outburst or expressive discharge.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Unhappy that I am, I cannot heave\\nMy heart into my mouth: I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":2,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.88,\"reason\":\"Beyond avowing love and invoking filial obligation, the expression performs a corrective refusal: Cordelia resists Lear's competitive demand for an expansive declaration and limits her claim to what she considers the proper measure of filial love.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Which of you shall we say doth love us most?\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"According to my bond; no more nor less.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Why have my sisters husbands if they say\\nThey love you all?\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"Lear publicly asks his daughters to say which loves him most, linking their answers to his distribution of the kingdom. After initially answering “Nothing,” Cordelia gives this restrained avowal, defining her love by her filial bond rather than competing in hyperbolic praise.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"Cordelia makes no distinct new promise in the focal words. She invokes an existing daughter-to-father bond and identifies obedience, love, and honour as duties she returns to Lear. Lear rejects the answer as inadequate and urges her to revise it, so there is no uptake of it as the declaration he demanded.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied text is recognisable as William Shakespeare’s King Lear, specifically the opening love-test scene and later scenes involving Cordelia and Lear.\",\"limitations\":\"The evidence strongly supports genuine filial affection through Cordelia’s later care, grief, kiss, and concern for Lear. It does not provide a precise contractual content for her “bond,” and the focal expression itself does not establish a new undertaking.\"}"
        }
